  "article": {
    "hero": {
      "title": "🍔 Diner Name Generator",
      "tagline": "Names for an American-style diner, breakfast spot or roadside grill, from Chrome Cherry Diner to Miles & Pies."
    },
    "intro": "A diner name has to look good in neon and sound right when someone says \"meet you at…\": <em>Chrome Cherry Diner</em>, <em>Miles &amp; Pies</em>, <em>Short Stack Café</em>. This **diner name generator** gives you names in seven styles, from 50s retro and roadside to breakfast, family and modern, plus names built from your own name or your town.",
    "sections": [
      {
        "heading": "Eight good diner names",
        "content": "<p>All eight came from the generator:</p><ul><li><strong>Chrome Cherry Diner</strong>: two things every 50s diner had, the shiny counter and the cherry on the milkshake.</li><li><strong>Root Beer &amp; Records</strong>: promises floats and a jukebox before you walk in.</li><li><strong>Miles &amp; Pies</strong>: a rhyme for a roadside stop, and it tells drivers there's pie.</li><li><strong>Exit 14 Diner</strong>: named after the turn-off, so nobody gets lost finding it.</li><li><strong>Short Stack Café</strong>: diner slang for a small pile of pancakes, and a clear breakfast promise.</li><li><strong>Rise &amp; Dine</strong>: an easy pun for a place that opens early.</li><li><strong>Harper's Family Diner</strong>: a family name and \"family diner\" say \"bring the kids\" in three words.</li><li><strong>Griddle &amp; Rye</strong>: a modern pairing for a diner with a short, careful menu.</li></ul>"
      },
      {
        "heading": "What makes a diner name work",
        "content": "<p>Diners grew out of the night lunch wagons of Providence, Rhode Island, in the 1870s, which sold sandwiches, pie and coffee to night workers. Later, diners were built in factories and delivered by rail or road, long and narrow like a railway dining car, and the word <em>diner</em> is usually explained as short for dining car. That history is why so many diner names still sound like a place on the road.</p><ul><li><strong>Put \"Diner\" in it.</strong> The word tells people to expect booths, a counter, breakfast all day and burgers, so most styles keep it in the name.</li><li><strong>Pick an era or a promise.</strong> Choose <strong>50s retro</strong> for chrome, jukeboxes and milkshakes, <strong>Roadside</strong> for highway and truck stop names, or <strong>Breakfast</strong> if pancakes and fry-ups are the point.</li><li><strong>Say it in a sentence.</strong> \"Meet you at the Jukebox Diner\" works; a name you have to spell out loses customers.</li><li><strong>UK or US?</strong> In the UK a \"diner\" usually means the American style. The breakfast names include British ones, like <em>Bacon Butty Café</em>, for a place that serves a full English.</li></ul>"
      },
      {
        "heading": "Check it's free before you print the sign",
        "content": "<p>A name from a generator is a starting point, not permission to use it. Before you order the sign, menus or booking page:</p><ul><li><strong>Company register.</strong> In the UK, search Companies House; you can't register a limited company with the same name as an existing one. In the US, search your state's business register.</li><li><strong>Trade marks.</strong> Search the UK trade mark register (or the USPTO in the US) for the goods and services you'll sell.</li><li><strong>Domain and handles.</strong> Check the .co.uk or .com and the Instagram and TikTok handles you'll need.</li><li><strong>Your area.</strong> Search a map app and the delivery apps for businesses with the same or a similar name nearby.</li></ul><p>Press <strong>Check</strong> on any name, or use the <a href=\"../business-name-checker.html\">Business Name Checker</a>, to run most of these at once. For the full process, read our guide, <a href=\"../guides/business-naming-best-practices.html\">Business Naming Best Practices</a>. This is general guidance, not legal advice.</p><p>In the UK, any food business must register with the local council at least 28 days before it starts trading. Registration is free, and the council will inspect you and give you a food hygiene rating.</p>"
      },
      {
        "heading": "Tips for diner names",
        "content": "<ul><li><strong>Watch out for chain names.</strong> Some first names already belong to diner and burger chains, so if you choose <strong>Your name</strong>, press Check before you fall for one.</li><li><strong>Think about the sign.</strong> Diners are known for big neon and chrome lettering, and short names fit it best.</li><li><strong>Leave room on the menu.</strong> A name like <em>Hash Brown Diner</em> is fun, but it makes it hard to be known for anything else later.</li></ul><p>For a smarter place, try the <a href=\"restaurant-name-generator.html\">Restaurant Name Generator</a>; for coffee and cake, the <a href=\"cafe-name-generator.html\">Café Name Generator</a>; and for burgers on wheels, the <a href=\"food-truck-name-generator.html\">Food Truck Name Generator</a>.</p><p>For more, see all the <a href=\"../categories/biz_food-names.html\">food and drink business name generators</a>.</p>"
      }
    ],
    "faqs": [
      {
        "question": "What are good diner names?",
        "answer": "Short names that say \"diner\" and set the mood: Chrome Cherry Diner, Miles & Pies, Short Stack Café, Harper's Family Diner or Griddle & Rye. Choose a style, from 50s retro to roadside, breakfast, family or modern."
      },
      {
        "question": "How do I check a diner name is free?",
        "answer": "Press <strong>Check</strong> on any name to see whether it's free as a company name, a domain and a social media handle. Before you commit, search the official registers too: in the UK, Companies House and the UK trade mark register; in the US, your state's business register and the USPTO trade mark search. Look on a map app for businesses with the same name nearby."
      },
      {
        "question": "What is a good name for a 50s diner?",
        "answer": "Use the things people picture: jukeboxes, chrome, milkshakes and sock hops. The Jukebox Diner, Sock Hop Malt Shop or Hot Rod Diner & Grill all fit. Choose <strong>50s retro</strong> for more."
      },
      {
        "question": "What should I call a breakfast diner?",
        "answer": "Name the breakfast: Sunny Side Up Pancake House, Rise & Shine Brunch Bar or Early Bird Diner. Choose <strong>Breakfast</strong> for American pancake names and British fry-up names."
      },
      {
        "question": "Can I name a diner after myself?",
        "answer": "Yes, and it's a diner tradition. Choose <strong>Your name</strong> and type your first name for names like Sarah's Corner Diner or Sarah's Pancake House, then press Check in case a chain already uses your name."
      },
